# Requirements — Statement review: every figure traced, the unclear lines first

Bet: statement-review, signed 2026-11-16. Scope is a showcase for one firm (D15, D24): a working model the underwriter can try, after which the firm's approver decides on a pilot (D14, D25). Forms and risk tags follow the template.

Revision 33, after D315 (changes requested on 2026-11-21, the owner's decision of 2026-11-20 given in chat, after T17's eleventh review, verify-197; the owner had answered revision 32's question as D309, and D310 approved revision 32). Only P5 changes; its id, form and risk tag do not. D309 kept revision 32's default: a supplied policy's own text stays within P5, and T17 keeps its mask. T17's tenth review, verify-195, then found a custom rule's limits reaching the prompt in its requirement and detail cells, which the owner's 97e4026 masks as well. verify-197, T17's eleventh review, judged 97e4026 and failed P5 because the prompt's period line writes the statement's first and last dates, and a description can hold the same date: with the first date 2026-09-30 and 2026-09-30 in a transaction description, every prompt holds the description's string, under the shipped policy as under a custom one. Revision 32's scope excepted the text the tool writes into every prompt, and said that a figure's value and a rule's outcome are not among it, since the case decides them. No build can keep the period's dates out of a prompt that R30 lets carry the period. D315 accepts this as an edge case outside the guarantee, to push development forward, and asks for one scope limit under P5, keeping everything else in revision 32. R30 lets every prompt carry the period and the metric and rule tables, whose values the case decides: the period's dates, each figure's value and each rule's outcome. A string of the case's descriptions or profile that coincides with one of those values as the prompt writes it is the case's figure, not line-level data, and is no sentinel for P5; otherwise P5 and R30 contradict each other, since a prompt must carry the period. P5 is about text reaching a prompt from a description or profile field, which stays a sentinel. P5's scope of revision 33 states it. Revision 33 makes four readings. First, the values the case decides are read wherever the prompt writes them: in the tables, and in the sentence the policy check writes for each rule, which quotes the figure's value. A figure's value includes n/a, and a rule's outcome includes the overall outcome, which the rule table carries as a row. Second, 'coincides with one of those values as the prompt writes it' is read as also reaching a string that the prompt holds only within such a value, or only across such values and the text the tool writes beside them, such as 2026-09 within 2026-09-30, or Period: 2026-09-30. D315's reason holds for those strings too. Read as equality alone, 2026-09 in a description would stay a sentinel that every prompt for that statement holds, and P5 and R30 would contradict each other again; revision 33 asks the owner. Third, the text the tool writes is read, for P5, as everything the prompt holds that the tool, not the case, wrote. That is revision 32's list, together with the labels the prompt sets before its lines (Account:, Period:, Metrics table:, Policy table: and the redraft line's label), the fixed sentences the tool chooses among for a figure's method and writes around a value in a rule's sentence, and the text a custom rule's row shows in place of its own ((custom id), (custom rule), (custom requirement)). Revision 32's list did not name them, because a method the case leads to and a custom rule's masks are not in every prompt, and a label is not a table's heading. Without them, a description holding Period: or (custom rule) would hold a sentinel that only the tool's own text puts in the prompt. Fourth, read with the scope of revision 32, P5 then asks that a prompt hold nothing but the text the tool writes and the values the case decides, which is D315's last sentence: a string of the case's descriptions or profile that a prompt holds anywhere else stays a sentinel, as a string a supplied policy's own text holds does (D309). verify-197's three majors on R29 and P18, and T04's reviews since revision 32, verify-196 and verify-198, which failed